 ### [Hybrid Voxel Rendering (Mesh/Splatting)](5766-hybrid-voxel-rendering-mesh-splatting.md)
-Uses splatting for distant voxels and mesh rendering for nearby ones to balance performance and quality.
+Combines OpenGL splatting for distant voxels with mesh rendering for nearby voxels to reduce vertex count and mitigate slow fragment shader performance inherent to pure splatting when close to the camera.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel generates one point primitive. The cost is dominated by the vertex shader and geometry shader (if used for sprite expansion) [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the voxel data. The packed format (32-bit per voxel) is memory efficient [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Highly dependent on the number of active voxels and the complexity of the fragment shader (e.g., quadric projection) [S1, unofficial]. Stochastic culling significantly improves performance for distant views by reducing the number of fragments processed [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Low CPU overhead for geometry generation; simple data structure; effective for large, dense grids when combined with mipmapping and culling [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Transparency handling can be complex (requires back-to-front sorting or alpha blending); point sprites may alias or appear pixelated if not properly sized; performance degrades with very high-resolution voxels close to the camera due to fragment shader cost [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time visualization of large voxel datasets where approximate volume representation is sufficient, and CPU-side mesh generation is a bottleneck [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When precise geometric boundaries are required, or when transparency sorting is too expensive for the target frame rate [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of voxels $N$, $O(N)$, as each voxel generates a single point primitive [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to $N$, storing packed voxel data in buffers [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Performance is heavily dependent on fragment shader cost for large point sprites. Pure splatting can suffer from overdraw and expensive fragment processing when voxels are close to the camera [S1, unofficial]. Stochastic culling [S1, unofficial] and mipmapping [S2, unofficial] are used to mitigate this.
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation using standard OpenGL point primitives; efficient for large, distant volumes; low vertex count compared to mesh-based voxel rendering.
+*   **Limitations:** Poor performance for close-up views due to fragment shader load; potential aliasing artifacts if point sizing is not carefully managed; requires geometry shader or specific point sprite handling for proper 3D appearance [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Rendering large-scale volumetric data where distant details are less critical, or as a fallback for simple visualization.
+*   **When not to use:** High-fidelity close-up rendering where mesh geometry [5766-hybrid-voxel-rendering-mesh-splatting.md] or ray-traced methods are preferred.
 
 ## Background
-Voxel Point Sprite Rendering is an implementation of the broader splatting technique applied to voxel data. It leverages OpenGL's point sprite capabilities to render voxels as screen-aligned quads or circles. The specific implementations cited originate from open-source experiments in high-performance voxel rendering [S1, S2, unofficial]. The technique is part of the evolution of point-based rendering methods, adapting the concept of splatting from surface reconstruction to volumetric data visualization.
+Voxel Point Sprite Rendering is an unofficial technique demonstrated in open-source experiments [S1, S2, unofficial]. It leverages OpenGL's point sprite capabilities and geometry shaders to render volumetric data efficiently. The approach was explored in repositories such as `clayjohn/voxel-renderer` [S1, unofficial] and `sp4cerat/OpenGL-Voxel-Splatting` [S2, unofficial] as alternatives to traditional mesh-based voxel rendering.
 
